8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the input is made of.** Each input is a large, regular tiling. Divider lines in the background colour (8, 5, 5 and 4 in the four grids) cut it into cells, and a repeating pattern of small colours fills those cells. One thing breaks the regularity: a hollow square outline in a colour that is otherwise rare or absent.\n\n- Pair 1: a 2-frame at rows/cols 2–8.\n- Pair 2: a 3-frame at 5–11. It is drawn over the 5-dividers.\n- Pair 3: a 6-frame at rows 17–23, cols 5–11. It also replaces divider cells.\n\n**What the output is.** The fact sheet says each output is an exact crop of the input, at (2,2), (5,5) and (17,5). Those are exactly the top-left corners of the outlines, and each output is 7×7, the size of the outline. So the rule is: **find the hollow rectangular frame and return the input cropped to it, frame included.** Nothing inside the frame changes.\n\n**How to detect the frame in general.** For every colour, I take its 4-connected components. A component is the frame if:\n- its bounding box is at least 3×3, and\n- its cells are exactly the border cells of that bounding box.\n\nThe repeating motifs do not pass this test. The 2×2 blocks of 0 are too small. The colour-4 crosses in pair 2 are not borders of their boxes. Pieces of divider lines are only one cell thick, so they fail the size test.\n\n**Test input.** The odd outline is colour 8 at rows/cols 14–19. It is a 6×6 frame of 20 cells, which is exactly the perimeter of a 6×6 box. The output is therefore that 6×6 crop: the 8-border plus the 4×4 patch of tiling inside it. The crop size is read from the frame, so it does not matter that it differs from the 7×7 train outputs.\n\n**Emitted stages.** The task is a single crop. I emit one canvas, the cropped frame, which is also the final answer.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    best = None\n    for c in np.unique(g):\n        for comp in components(g == c):\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(comp)\n            h, w = y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1\n            if h < 3 or w < 3:\n                continue\n            per = 2 * (h + w) - 4\n            if len(comp) != per:\n                continue\n            if all(y in (y0, y1) or x in (x0, x1) for y, x in comp):\n                if best is None or per > best[0]:\n                    best = (per, (y0, x0, y1, x1), int(c))\n    _, (y0, x0, y1, x1), c = best\n    out = crop(g, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n    emit(out, f\"Found the hollow frame of colour {c} at rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1}; crop the input to it.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How to detect the frame in general.** For every colour, I take its 4-connected components."}],"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Divider lines in the background colour (8, 5, 5 and 4 in the four grids) cut it into cells, and a repeating pattern of small colours fills those cells."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is drawn over the 5-dividers."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It also replaces divider cells."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the input is made of.** Each input is a large, regular tiling."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Divider lines in the background colour (8, 5, 5 and 4 in the four grids) cut it into cells, and a repeating pattern of small colours fills those cells."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The output is therefore that 6×6 crop: the 8-border plus the 4×4 patch of tiling inside it."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output is.** The fact sheet says each output is an exact crop of the input, at (2,2), (5,5) and (17,5)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is: **find the hollow rectangular frame and return the input cropped to it, frame included.** Nothing inside the frame changes."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- its bounding box is at least 3×3, and"}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the input is made of.** Each input is a large, regular tiling."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The output is therefore that 6×6 crop: the 8-border plus the 4×4 patch of tiling inside it."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"cccf7e7066536e68dcae97712b92f4579db7d00fcee2c4bd83c0eadd6532e638","task_sha256":"361b0002aa03a4683a95a63eba0c9ba1730b45d951db1cd88aafcc57f14d11ac","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
